# Collection Integration

Choose exactly one build-needs lane, stopping at the first matching rule:

0. Without a settings file, plugin, or patch, keep the package out of builder
   collections. Combined onboarding provides a builder override.
1. If bootstrapping reaches torch (including build/install dependencies,
   libtorch/libc10 linkage, or torch-pinned companions such as triton), use a
   `torch-*` collection. Map each relevant onboarding job's `TORCH_VERSION`
   through `rhai_pipeline.torch_versions[version].builder_collection` in the
   root `ci-job-definitions.yml`. Verify its CPU constraints pin `torch==`;
   never select a collection by sorting for the highest version.
2. If every wheel we build is `py3-none-any`, use `universal/cpu-ubi9`, built
   once on x86_64. Judge the source build, not only the wheel published on PyPI.
3. If the build differs per accelerator, or pulls in a package whose build
   does, use `torchless/<accelerator variant>` and also `torchless/cpu-ubi9`
   when there is a CPU build.
4. Otherwise use `torchless/cpu-ubi9`. Loading accelerator libraries only at
   runtime does not make an otherwise identical build accelerator-specific.

Never put a package that pulls torch or triton in universal or torchless.
Use the existing collections and variants. Add requirements to
`requirements.txt` or a file under `requirements/`, preserving markers and
tracking comments. Build from source; never generate `pre_built: true`.
